<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dolforwyn Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1273</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dolforwyn Castle is a ruined Welsh castle in Powys, mid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> from 1273 above the Severn valley near Montgomery. Edward I forbade its construction, and the dispute added to the tensions that led to war in 1277, when English forces besieged and captured it. It was among the last castles built by a native Welsh prince.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dolforwyn-castle.jpg" alt="Dolforwyn Castle" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mr M Evison · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dolforwyn_Castle,_Powys_-_geograph.org.uk_-_404113.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
